---
alwaysApply: true
---

Project identity

- Module / target name: cstatiWarehouse.
- In file headers, always use exactly `cstatiWarehouse` as the project name.
- User-facing UI text and date formatting must be in Russian.
- Prefer `ru_RU` locale for user-visible date formatting unless the existing code explicitly requires something else.

File header

- Every Swift file must start with a header comment in this exact format:

//
//  {FileName}.swift
//  cstatiWarehouse
//
//  Created by Артём on {DD.MM.YYYY}.
//

- `{FileName}` must match the actual file name.
- Date must always be the current date at the time of file creation.
- Keep the exact spacing and line breaks.
- When substantially editing an existing file with an outdated header, normalize it to this format.

Architecture

- Feature scenes must follow this structure:
  - Assembly
  - Presenter
  - Interactor
  - Router
  - SwiftUI View
- Entity may live next to the scene when it is tightly related to that scene.

- New scenes should follow the same folder and file structure pattern as Login / Register.
- Keep scene structure consistent across the project.

Navigation

- Navigation is allowed only through Router + AppCoordinatorProtocol.
- Do not introduce an alternative navigation path directly from View.
- Do not create a second navigation mechanism for the same flow.
- New root-level navigation decisions must stay aligned with RootView / coordinator flow.

App shell

- Entry flow must remain:
  - CstatiWarehouseApp
  - RootView
  - splash flow
  - CoordinatorView with NavigationStack and AppCoordinator
- Do not replace this flow with a separate WindowGroup-based root approach unless explicitly required.

SwiftUI / Observation

- Use `@Observable` Presenter for screen logic.
- Use `@Bindable` in View.
- Do not mix this with a different state management style without a strong reason.
- Keep screen state minimal, explicit, and predictable.

Scene conventions

- Presenter startup must follow the existing LoginAssembly pattern:
  - trigger `presenter.viewDidLoad()` from the assembled View’s `.onAppear`
  - do not move that startup logic into View init.

- Interactor should keep:
  - `weak var presenter` where applicable
  - `[weak self]` in service callbacks where needed to avoid retain cycles.

- Boundary protocol naming must stay consistent with existing scenes:
  - `...InteractorInputProtocol`
  - `...InteractorOutputProtocol`
  - `...PresenterProtocol`
  - `...RouterProtocol`

Layer responsibilities

- View:
  - UI rendering only
  - binds to Presenter
  - no navigation logic except through Router/coordinator flow
  - no business logic

- Presenter:
  - screen state
  - presentation logic
  - formatting for View
  - may coordinate UI-triggered flow
  - no networking
  - no direct navigation bypassing Router

- Interactor:
  - business logic
  - service calls
  - data fetching / mutations
  - no UI code

- Router:
  - navigation only
  - uses AppCoordinatorProtocol
  - no business logic
  - no UI formatting logic

- Assembly:
  - wires dependencies
  - builds the scene
  - keeps dependency graph explicit

Data layers

- API request/response DTO models and auth-related API errors should live next to the service contract where that pattern already exists.
- Do not move DTOs into Entity/ without a clear reason.
- Domain models in `Entity/` must stay separate from API DTOs.
- Do not merge domain model and API DTO into one type without explicit mapping.
- Keep explicit mapping between DTOs and domain entities when both layers exist.

Services

- Follow the existing service pattern:
  - protocol
  - mock implementation
  - real API implementation
- Example pattern:
  - `AuthServiceProtocol`
  - `MockAuthService`
  - `ApiAuthService`
- New services should follow the same structure.

Concurrency

- Current services may use completion handlers.
- When implementing or extending services, make main-thread delivery explicit.
- One of these must be clearly guaranteed:
  - completion is called on main thread
  - or Presenter/UI update path is explicitly isolated to main thread
- Any update of user-visible Presenter state or navigation-triggering state must happen safely on main thread.
- Avoid implicit thread assumptions that may cause SwiftUI update warnings or glitches.

UIKit ↔ SwiftUI bridges

- If bridging UIKit into SwiftUI, follow the existing pattern used for Lottie:
  - small dedicated wrapper type
  - `UIViewRepresentable`
  - minimal logic inside `makeUIView` / `updateUIView`
- Do not place business logic inside representable wrappers.

Visual style

- Do not introduce a third visual style if the project already has an established one.
- For auth screens, follow the existing style:
  - `AnimatedGradientBackground`
  - `GlassTextField`
  - glass-style buttons
  - typography via `CustomFonts` / font extensions
- For other screens and cards, prefer the existing background/styling approach already used in the project.
- Prefer `CustomColors`, `Color.adaptive`, or shared color helpers from `CustomColors.swift`.
- Do not introduce arbitrary new hex colors in each file unless there is a clear design exception.
- Do not default to `.system` fonts/colors if the existing screen style uses custom typography/colors.
- Keep new screens visually consistent with existing app patterns.

Preview

- For component previews, prefer a readable background consistent with the project’s visual style.
- For scene previews that require coordinator wiring, follow the existing assembly-based preview style with a local `AppCoordinator()`.

Tab / placeholders

- In tab flows, prefer replacing an existing placeholder with a real screen rather than creating a second tab or duplicate mock flow unnecessarily.
- Keep placeholder usage explicit and temporary.

File organization

- Prefer one primary type per file.
- If a file contains only one main type, do not add a MARK above that type itself.
- If a file contains multiple top-level entities, separate them with:
  - `// MARK: - SectionName`
- Closely related domain types may live in one file, but then MARK separation between top-level entities is required.

Top-level file order

- Prefer this order inside a file:
  1. Primary type
  2. Closely related helper types
  3. Extensions for protocol conformances

MARK rules

- Inside a single type or extension, use:
  - `// MARK: SectionName`
- Between top-level entities in one file, use:
  - `// MARK: - SectionName`
- Keep MARK naming concise and consistent.
- Do not create empty MARK sections.
- Remove unused sections.

Type member ordering

Inside a type, use this order:

1. Nested types
- enums
- structs
- typealiases

2. Properties
- dependencies
- state
- UI properties
- computed properties

3. Lifecycle

4. Public Methods

5. Private Methods

6. Actions

7. UI Configuration

Properties

- Prefer `private` by default.
- Use `private(set)` when external read access is needed.
- Do not broaden access level without a clear reason.
- Group properties by responsibility, not randomly.

Lifecycle

- Prefer lifecycle methods over heavy init logic where applicable.
- Keep lifecycle methods short and focused.
- For SwiftUI-based scene flow, respect the existing `.onAppear` startup pattern instead of moving startup behavior into init.

Standard MARK layout for scene/view-oriented types

// MARK: Properties
// MARK: Lifecycle
// MARK: Public Methods
// MARK: Private Methods
// MARK: Actions
// MARK: UI Configuration

Interactor / Presenter / Router / Service layout

// MARK: Properties
// MARK: Public Methods
// MARK: Private Methods

Methods

- Prefer top-down readability:
  - entry points first
  - high-level flow next
  - low-level helpers later
- In Public Methods, place the main API first.
- In Private Methods, place methods used by lifecycle/public methods first.

Actions

- Actions are only UI event handlers.
- Keep them thin.
- Do not place business logic directly inside actions.
- Delegate real work to Presenter / Interactor / private helper methods according to layer responsibility.

UI Configuration

- Group UI setup logic under UI Configuration.
- Prefer this order where applicable:
  1. `setupViews()`
  2. `setupHierarchy()`
  3. `setupConstraints()`
  4. `setupAppearance()`
  5. `setupBindings()`

Extensions

- Use extensions primarily for protocol conformances.
- Prefer one protocol conformance per extension when reasonable.
- Do not group unrelated protocols into one extension.
- Keep extension order logical:
  1. architecture protocols
  2. SwiftUI / UIKit protocols
  3. other supporting protocols

Formatting

- Use 4 spaces for indentation in new and modified code.
- When editing files with mixed indentation, normalize touched areas toward 4 spaces.
- Keep formatting consistent within the touched file.

Consistency rules

- Prefer consistency with the existing project/module structure over introducing a new pattern.
- Do not refactor unrelated code just to match a preferred style.
- When the repository already has an established local convention, prefer extending that convention instead of inventing a parallel one.
